  "scenario": "A buyer in their mid-thirties wants one camera system that can still be useful when their children graduate, is willing to spend more to avoid regret, and has heard that shutters, weather seals and lens mounts wear. The frozen shopping packet contains a Pentax K-30 body page whose title says discontinued while a later page field says it is not discontinued, a Pentax K-3 Mark III body page, a Pentax K-1 Mark II body bundle, a Nikon D7500 body bundle, an international-version Canon 5D Mark IV body page and a weather-sealed Rokinon Canon-EF lens page. Frozen model pages map the K-3 Mark III to a 2021 Pentax KAF2 interchangeable-lens DSLR, the D7500 to a 2017 Nikon F-mount DSLR and the 5D Mark IV to a Canon EF full-frame DSLR announced in 2016. General pages define focal-plane shutters, lens mounts, O-rings, gaskets, reliability, MTBF, spare parts and field-replaceable units. Three community pages contain only scoped anecdotes or questions about donating old good-condition cameras, finding vintage lenses locally and obtaining replacement laptop parts. No captured source supplies model-specific shutter survival distributions, exact-unit shutter counts, camera seal material or maintenance schedules, mount-wear measurements, current authorized-service and parts commitments, a decade-long controlled comparison or a universally most durable body and lens.",
