  "rules": {
    "exact_offer_condition_weather_claim_scope_v1": "The six shopping pages are frozen seller assertions, not current delivered offers, independent durability tests or standardized ingress certifications. The K-30 title calls the white body-only model discontinued, while the same page later says Is Discontinued By Manufacturer No; retain that conflict. The page shows SKU B0082OJ2YG at 359.49 dollars, rated 73 percent over twelve reviews. The K-3 Mark III page shows SKU B091TTFCCH at 1,996.95 dollars, rated 93 percent over eleven reviews. The K-1 Mark II page shows SKU B07L2BQV81 at 1,602.05 dollars with no reviews shown. The D7500 page shows SKU B08N5G61P2 at 1,199 dollars with no reviews shown and describes an import-model bundle with USA-compatible accessories and a one-year limited seller warranty. The 5D Mark IV page shows SKU B01LY3WID1 at 2,499 dollars with no reviews shown and calls itself an international version. The Rokinon page shows SKU B0898Z5FVG at 349 dollars with no reviews shown and says the weather-sealed lens mount protects the gap between lens and camera mount. None verifies current availability, exact delivered contents, exact-unit condition, shutter count, seal health, mount play, authorized warranty or decade survival.",
    "model_release_mount_mapping_v1": "The frozen model pages map the Pentax K-3 Mark III to a Pentax KAF2 interchangeable-lens DSLR released in 2021, the Nikon D7500 to a Nikon F-mount interchangeable-lens DSLR announced in 2017 and the Canon 5D Mark IV to an interchangeable Canon EF full-frame DSLR announced in 2016. The pages also describe focal-plane shutters. These are model-level identity and interface facts, not evidence that one captured seller unit is genuine, healthy, supported or more likely to survive ten years.",
    "shutter_mount_wear_boundary_v1": "A focal-plane shutter is positioned immediately in front of the film or image sensor and traditional forms use moving curtains. A lens mount is a mechanical and often electrical interface between body and lens. Those definitions identify components to inspect, but the packet gives no model-specific actuation rating, failure distribution, repair quote, mount-tolerance measurement, abuse history or exact-unit remaining life. Release year, burst rate and focal-plane-shutter presence cannot be converted into a survival ranking.",
    "seal_mechanism_maintenance_boundary_v1": "An O-ring is an elastomer loop compressed in a groove to form a seal, and a gasket fills space between mating surfaces under compression. Material selection and operating conditions matter. These generic mechanisms explain why seal condition can depend on material, compression, contamination, temperature and service, but no captured page identifies every seal in a candidate camera or lens, its material, maintenance schedule, replacement availability or current condition. Weather-marketing language therefore defines a verification target rather than proving decade-long protection.",
    "reliability_metric_exact_unit_boundary_v1": "Reliability is the probability that a product or system performs an intended function for a specified period or in a defined environment. MTBF is an average or predicted time between inherent failures for a repairable system, depends on the failure definition and is distinct from MTTF for a non-repairable system. A useful estimate requires a defined population, operating profile, censoring and repair policy. The packet contains none of those model-specific distributions, so generic reliability or MTBF language cannot predict one used camera or rank the bodies.",
    "spare_parts_field_replaceability_boundary_v1": "A spare part is an interchangeable part held for repair or refurbishment, while a field-replaceable unit can be removed and replaced without sending the entire system to a repair facility; FRU granularity affects total ownership and support cost. These concepts do not prove that a camera shutter, seal, mount, board or battery is sold, documented, authorized, economical or available in the buyer's region. Physical durability, repairability, parts access and economic serviceability must remain separate columns.",
    "community_lifetime_service_scope_v1": "One Richmond author says several old point-and-shoot cameras and a GoPro are in good condition with accessories and asks where to donate them. One Baltimore author asks where to find vintage camera lenses and receives local, mixed suggestions. One Dell XPS owner reports obtaining a barrel jack and keyboard after identifying parts and discussing self-repair. These anecdotes can motivate condition, secondary-market and parts-channel questions, but they are not camera survival distributions, verified inventories, authorized camera repair policies or promises of future supply.",
    "decade_ownership_verification_trial_v1": "For every exact candidate unit and lens, verify serial and regional identity, seller and delivered contents, condition, shutter count and control operation; inspect sensor, shutter behavior, mount play, contacts, ports, card slots, battery, charger, weather-seal surfaces and lens optics, focus and aperture. Ask the current manufacturer or authorized service channel in writing whether the region accepts the exact serial and can quote shutter, seal, mount, board and other likely repairs, what parts are available and for how long, and what work is field or depot replaceable. Use an independent inspection and a return-compliant or rental trial in the buyer's actual shooting and environmental profile. Record failures and unresolved fields instead of extrapolating from marketing.",
    "ownership_cost_serviceability_matrix_v1": "Build an exact-system table that separates frozen seller claims, model facts, exact-unit inspection, current service answers, trial outcomes and unresolved items. Include body, compatible lenses, batteries, media, preventive maintenance, expected inspection and repair scenarios, downtime and replacement fallback in total ownership cost. Mark identity, condition, functional fit, compatibility, seal, service, parts, maintenance, downtime and budget gates pass, fail or unresolved. Price, sensor size, release year, weather wording, store rating and anecdotes may direct verification but cannot override a failed gate.",
    "evidence_bounded_decade_camera_decision_v1": "The packet proves no universal buy-it-for-life body or lens. Choose only the lowest total-ownership exact system that passes predeclared use-profile, identity, condition, compatibility, function, maintenance, current regional serviceability, parts and budget gates after inspection and a reversible trial. Scope the conclusion to the exact serials, lenses, environment, service answers and decision date. If no system passes or material fields remain unresolved, keep or repair current equipment, rent, buy a smaller more replaceable system, or defer. Do not promise decade survival."
